---
title: Irish Stew (Instant Pot)
description: Brothy, potato-forward Irish stew adapted for the Instant Pot, using the requested rump steak.
source: Original blend — see the source-blend note at the end of the file
servings: 6
time required: 1 hour 10 minutes
prep time: 15 minutes
cook time: 45 minutes
course: main
cuisine: Irish
diet: nut-free
tags: stew, irish, instant-pot, beef, pressure-cooker, comfort-food, main
---

Season @rump steak{800%g}(trimmed, cut into ~3 cm chunks, patted dry) all
over with @sea salt{1%tsp} and @black pepper{1/2%tsp}.

Set the #Instant Pot{} to Sauté, add @olive oil{2%tbsp} and brown the beef
in two batches for ~{7%minutes}, turning until deeply crusted. Transfer the
brown meat to a #plate{} -- no need to rest it, it goes back in soon.

Drop in @onion{2}(quartered and sliced) and Sauté for ~{4%minutes} until
soft at the edges, then add @garlic{3%cloves}(crushed) for ~{1%minute}
more — scrape the crust off the base with the garlic, splashing in stock
from the measured amount if it threatens to catch.

Add the @beef stock{500%ml}, @thyme{4%sprigs},
@bay leaves{2} and another grind of pepper. Lock the lid, valve to Sealing,
and cook on High Pressure for ~{15%minutes}. Let the pressure ease off
naturally for ~{10%minutes}, then quick-release the rest. Rump is leaner
than the chuck and lamb used in many stew recipes, so this shorter first
stage is a cautious starting point to keep it from becoming dry and stringy.

Nestle @potatoes{750%g}(peeled, cut into ~3 cm chunks) and
@carrots{3}(peeled, cut into ~2 cm logs) on top of the meat — don't stir
them under. Lock the lid and cook on High Pressure for a further ~{4%minutes},
then allow a ~{10%minutes} natural release before opening. The short second
stage is intended to cook the vegetables while limiting how long the rump
stays under pressure.

Mash a few potato chunks against the side of the pot and stir the stew —
the crushed potato thickens the broth without a separate thickener. Taste
and adjust @sea salt{} to taste. Stir through @flat leaf parsley{1/4%cup}(roughly chopped),
saving some to scatter on top.

Serve straight from the pot in deep bowls with bread for dunking.

> Swaps to consider: lamb shoulder is the traditional cut, but needs a longer pressure cook (use a tested lamb timing rather than the rump timing here). Replace 250 ml of stock with @?Guinness{250%ml} for a stout version, or add @?Worcestershire sauce{1%tbsp} for savoury depth. For a thicker gravy, mix @?cornflour{1%tbsp} with cold water and stir it in on Sauté until glossy.

> Source blend — this is an original adaptation, not a transcription. The AP's Irish lamb stew article supplied the potato-and-onion foundation and crushed-potato thickener; Amy + Jacky's Instant Pot Irish beef stew informed pressure-cooker layering and seasoning options; Instant Pot's official beef stew and Well Plated's Instant Pot beef stew informed sautéing, deglazing and pressure-cooker workflow. Their longer timings are for stew beef or chuck, so I shortened the meat stage as a cautious starting point for the requested lean rump; that rump timing still needs a kitchen trial. https://apnews.com/article/saint-patricks-recipe-irish-lamb-stew-be4b39bfc1f7a49a81c7b520b67a405b https://www.pressurecookrecipes.com/instant-pot-irish-beef-stew/ https://instantpot.com/blogs/recipes/basic-beef-stew https://www.wellplated.com/instant-pot-beef-stew/
